{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "a01",
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "score": 4,
      "estimated_minutes": 5,
      "reason": "The reviewer must check paragraph 3 and work through whether the description's contrast between authorized creation and 'unmodified derivatives' identifies the copied-versus-physical-material problem. Its additional emphasis on 'know-how or data' and the resulting construct's progeny requires separating the central complaint from its extensions."
    },
    {
      "id": "a02",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 1,
      "estimated_minutes": 1,
      "reason": "The description targets the handwritten inventorship addition and the license grant, rather than 'product that contains Material.' Those textual and assent objections are plainly separate from the court's copied-sequence-versus-physical-part ambiguity."
    },
    {
      "id": "a03",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "A quick check of paragraphs 1 and 4 identifies the asserted conflict between commercialization permission, the commercial-use prohibition, and reserved rights. Comparing that conflict with d02 does not require resolving whether the three provisions can actually be reconciled."
    },
    {
      "id": "a04",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The description questions the construct's identity because of its garbled designation, whereas d03 concerns the definition's reach to copied and modified material. The reviewer need not resolve the biological nomenclature to distinguish those complaints."
    },
    {
      "id": "a05",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 3,
      "estimated_minutes": 3,
      "reason": "One additional look at Sections 3 and 5 checks the quoted relationship between authorized production and derivative-product triggers. The description expressly identifies the central question whether the resulting lentiviral clone is itself 'Material,' making the comparison with d03 concrete."
    },
    {
      "id": "a06",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The reviewer can distinguish uncertainty about manufacture, trial use, sufficient quantities, and exhaustion of the permitted purpose from uncertainty about whether the resulting clone is itself 'Materials.' The shared clause does not obscure the different questions being raised."
    },
    {
      "id": "a07",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 3,
      "estimated_minutes": 3,
      "reason": "The reviewer needs one more look at Section 5 to distinguish the asserted incompatibility between Phase I use and commercialization from d04's classification of the resulting clone. Although both involve reading provisions together, the description states its alleged conflict explicitly."
    },
    {
      "id": "a08",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "score": 7,
      "estimated_minutes": 15,
      "reason": "The timing, procedure, and agreement-to-agree objections are readily separated, but the final sentence calls the court-construed 'but for' language an undefined trigger without identifying the copied-versus-physical-material issue. The difficult step is deciding whether that general assertion names d05's application-specific ambiguity or merely raises a different objection to the same words."
    }
  ]
}
